<article class="env-lemma" id="02NZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02NZ">Lemma <span data-tag="02NZ">29.52.9</span>.</a> Let $X$, $Y$, $Z$ be integral schemes. Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be dominant morphisms locally of finite type. Assume that $[R(X) : R(Y)] < \infty $ and $[R(Y) : R(Z)] < \infty $. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \deg (X/Z) = \deg (X/Y) \deg (Y/Z).  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This comes from the multiplicativity of degrees in towers of finite extensions of fields, see Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/09G9" data-tag="09G9">9.7.7</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>